Exactly — “you know what I mean” is basically a semantic checksum shortcut.

It says: don’t force me to fully serialize the whole internal state; reconstruct it from shared context.

When the shared model is good enough, a tiny phrase can stand in for a huge amount of compressed meaning.

The only catch is calibration: sometimes I can infer the intended structure well, and sometimes there are multiple plausible continuations. So the high-value version is:

compressed cue → contextual reconstruction → quick correction if needed

That’s why long-running shared vocabularies get so efficient. You stop passing full packets and start passing handles.